        "A wheelchair is a prescription. The PT considers diagnosis and prognosis, posture and range of motion, skin integrity and sensation, upper extremity strength and pain, cognition, transportation, home environment, and the user's goals. Choosing a chair that a user cannot propel, or that pushes him into a slumped posture, causes pressure injuries, shoulder pain, and loss of independence.",
        "<b>Manual versus power.</b> Manual chairs range from standard (heavy) to lightweight and <b>ultralight</b> rigid-frame chairs with adjustable axle positions. Ultralight chairs reduce the force and repetitions needed to propel, which protects the shoulders in long-term users such as those with paraplegia. Moving the rear axle forward improves maneuverability and propulsion efficiency but reduces rearward stability, so anti-tippers may be needed while skills develop. <b>Hemi-height</b> chairs have a lower seat so a person with hemiplegia can propel with the intact foot and hand. A <b>power wheelchair</b> is appropriate when the user cannot propel a manual chair functionally, has upper extremity pain or limited endurance, or needs power seating functions. Drive controls include joysticks, head arrays, and sip-and-puff controls.",
        "<b>Posture</b> starts at the pelvis. A seat that is too deep, a sling seat, or tight hamstrings drives the pelvis into <b>posterior tilt</b>, causing sacral sitting, kyphosis, and sacral and coccygeal pressure. A solid seat base and correct depth help. Pelvic obliquity and rotation shift load to one ischial tuberosity. Lateral trunk supports, headrests, and pelvic positioning belts (angled at about 45 degrees across the hips) support alignment. <b>Tilt-in-space</b> rotates the whole seat while keeping hip and knee angles constant, providing pressure redistribution without shear and without triggering extensor spasms. <b>Recline</b> opens the seat-to-back angle, which helps with catheterization, orthostatic hypotension, and rest, but can create shear and trigger spasms.",
        "<b>Typical measurements.</b> <b>Seat width</b>: widest point of the hips or thighs plus about 2 inches (about 1 inch each side). <b>Seat depth</b>: posterior buttock to the popliteal fossa minus about 2 to 3 inches, to avoid pressure behind the knee. <b>Seat height and footrest</b>: allow at least about 2 inches of clearance between the footplate and the floor and keep the thighs supported. <b>Armrest height</b>: seat to the elbow with the elbow flexed 90 degrees, plus about 1 inch. <b>Back height</b> depends on trunk control: lower backs for active users, higher backs with trunk or head support for those with poor control.",
        "<b>Cushions and pressure.</b> Foam cushions are light and inexpensive. Gel and contoured foam improve load distribution. <b>Air-cell</b> cushions provide excellent pressure redistribution for high-risk users but need regular inflation checks (a hand check under the ischial tuberosities). No cushion replaces <b>pressure relief</b>: users perform weight shifts (forward lean, side lean, push-up, or power tilt) frequently, commonly every 15 to 30 minutes, held long enough to restore perfusion. Pressure mapping can guide cushion selection.",
        "<b>Environmental access and the ADA.</b> The 2010 ADA Standards require ramps with a maximum slope of <b>1:12</b> (1 inch of rise for every 12 inches of run), a maximum rise of 30 inches per run, level landings at least 60 inches long, a minimum clear width of 36 inches, and handrails on both sides when the rise exceeds 6 inches. Doorways need at least 32 inches of clear width, and a 60-inch diameter clear floor space allows a wheelchair turn. Home modifications also include grab bars, roll-in showers, raised toilet seats, and lever handles. Other assistive technology includes reachers, environmental control units, and communication devices.",
